Supplementary 2. Taxonomic authority acquisition and digitization
Species-level circumscriptions from each authority were extracted and incorporated into the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) through a multi-stage workflow consisting of: (1) acquisition of taxonomic authorities through downloads, database exports, or digitization of printed resources; (2) conversion of authority-specific treatments into machine-readable formats; and (3) incorporation into a common framework for subsequent concept alignment and comparative analyses.
S2.1 Acquisition of taxonomic authorities
The effort required to acquire taxonomic treatments varied substantially across the selected authorities. Contemporary digital resources such as the Mammal Diversity Database (MDD) were available in tabular form and required relatively little reformatting prior to incorporation into the BTA. BatNames versions were acquired directly as CSV exports from the underlying database infrastructure, with version numbers and timestamps recorded for each download when available. Access to these backend exports was facilitated by a coauthor involved in the management of BatNames. In contrast, several other authorities existed only as printed resources and required substantial effort to digitize and convert into machine-readable formats (see S2.2).
The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species presents a particular challenge because individual records and database releases do not currently operate under a static versioning system. Consequently, a time-stamped archival approach was required to maintain reproducibility and ensure that taxonomic audits can be reconciled with the specific version of the Red List available at the time of analysis. Documenting these temporal snapshots was essential for evaluating ingestion gaps between the IUCN Red List and other authorities such as BatNames and the Mammal Diversity Database.
S2.2 Digitization of historical authorities and printed resources
Several foundational bat taxonomic references were originally published only in print form and have subsequently been digitized through community biodiversity informatics initiatives. Historical authorities including Corbet & Hill (1980, 1991), Mammal Species of the World (MSW) editions 1 and 2, and Koopman (1994) were incorporated into the BTA through the Hesperomys database (Zijlstra, 2025a), which provides structured digital representations of historical mammal taxonomies and maintains close alignment with the Mammal Diversity Database.
A more extensive workflow was required for Handbook of the Mammals of the World (HMW). Printed HMW volumes were digitized by Picturae, a company specializing in large-scale digitization of museum collections and scientific materials. Approximately 5,000 species treatment[a]s were converted into searchable PDF files. These PDFs were subsequently parsed and annotated using Plazi's GoldenGate mark-up editor (CITE) to generate XML files containing machine-readable descriptions of individual HMW treatments. The resulting treatments were uploaded to Plazi's Treatment Bank infrastructure and deposited as openly accessible taxonomic treatments (e.g., Burgin, 2019).
Digitized representations of the HMW treatments were subsequently downloaded from a versioned copy of Treatment Bank (Plazi Community, 2025) and converted into comma-separated value (CSV) files using custom scripts (NEED to publish https://jhpoelen.nl/hmw as a data publication on Zenodo or similar). These structured outputs enabled direct incorporation of HMW taxonomic treatments into the BTA framework. We recognize that relatively few organizations possess the combination of resources, technical expertise, infrastructure, and funding necessary to undertake such a time-intensive and specialized digitization workflow. Nevertheless, the process transformed an important taxonomic reference into a reusable digital resource for biodiversity informatics applications and future taxonomic syntheses. HMW treatments are now openly accessible under an open-access license (Agosti & Egloff, 2009), facilitating their reuse in resources such as the BTA.
 
 


 
